# Adreno 540 devfreq: leave polling_interval at its upstream 50 ms.
#
# This rule used to force it to 10 ms, reasoning that a 50 ms poll leaves the
# first frames of an animation at the 257 MHz floor. That problem is real and
# is already solved better, by waking the GPU at the top OPP on activity after
# an idle gap (aport 0070). Forcing 10 ms then BREAKS that solution, because
# the two share a number:
#
#   msm_devfreq_active() takes a PM QoS floor at the top OPP and
#   msm_devfreq_boost_work() releases it exactly polling_ms later
#   (msm_gpu_devfreq.c:277-283) -- and devfreq's polling_interval store writes
#   back into that same profile struct.
#
# So a 10 ms poll made the boost expire after 10 ms, against a 16.6 ms frame at
# 60 Hz. Every frame then boosted to 710 MHz, lapsed, fell to 257, and boosted
# again: a core PLL reprogram and a core rail move twice per frame, on a GPU
# with no GMU to do it out of band. At 50 ms the floor outlives the frame
# interval, the next frame re-arms it before it lapses, and the GPU simply
# stays at the top for as long as you keep interacting.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-20 on kernel #35, same boot, same Firefox scroll:
#   polling=10: 16585 devfreq transitions, 2303 direct 257->710 and 1896 back,
#               every intermediate OPP unused
#   polling=50: 151 transitions, 19 direct 257->710, intermediates in use,
#               19.7 s of residency at 710, and tk-fps.py held 59.7-59.9 fps
#               with 0 underruns where the recorded baseline for this device
#               was "60-71 repeatedly, 25-41 common, 3-14 frequent collapses"
#
# If a future session wants to retune this, the governor's own knobs are in
# debugfs already -- /sys/kernel/debug/dri/0/devfreq/{upthreshold,
# downdifferential,idle_clamp} -- so it needs no rebuild and no new code.

# Keep the GPU out of runtime suspend across short interaction pauses; msm's
# 250 ms default means every touch after a reading pause pays the resume +
# requantisation cost before the first frame.
#
# On `bind`, not `add`: the attribute is created by the driver core for every
# device, but writing it returns -EIO until the driver has called
# pm_runtime_use_autosuspend() (drivers/base/power/sysfs.c,
# autosuspend_delay_ms_store). The platform `add` event fires before probe, so
# it would fail exactly the way the tmpfiles.d version did.
ACTION=="bind", SUBSYSTEM=="platform", KERNEL=="5000000.gpu", \
  ATTR{power/autosuspend_delay_ms}="1000"

# UFS clock+gear scaling costs far more than it saves on this device. msm8998
# has no operating-points-v2, so ufs_qcom_freq_to_gear_speed() returns
# UFS_HS_DONT_CHANGE and ufshcd_scale_gear() takes its legacy path down to
# clk_scaling.min_gear, which ufs-qcom never overrides -- HS-G1. The link then
# idles at HS-G1 / 50 MHz, and simple_ondemand's 60 ms window with a 70 % busy
# threshold is never satisfied by app-launch-shaped I/O, so a short read runs
# at the floor and only reaches HS-G3 as it is finishing. Measured, 16 MB cold
# O_DIRECT bursts: 225 MB/s scaling vs 545 MB/s pinned; 4 KiB random reads
# 3234 us vs 2005 us. This is not a 6.18 regression -- 6.0 behaves identically.
#
# Idle power is still covered: clkgate_enable and auto_hibern8 (150 ms each)
# are untouched, and they are what actually gates the clocks when the link goes
# quiet. Gear/clock scaling on top of that is a second-order saving.
#
# Anchored on sda, not on the ufshc platform device: ufshcd_add_lus() creates
# clkscale_enable and only then calls scsi_scan_host(), so the block add event
# is the first one guaranteed to see the attribute. The platform add and bind
# events both fire before the async scan has created it.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="sda", \
  RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'echo 0 > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/ufshcd-qcom/1da4000.ufshc/clkscale_enable'"
